SM(349,{"n":"Numbers 1","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Numbers 1.mp3","d":2258.9,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[9.04,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.8,"Okay, we'll go ahead and get started."],[24.4,"We're gonna get started here in just a moment."],[27.68,"We're gonna be we're gonna be starting at the beginning of the book of Numbers."],[33.04,"Numbers 1 verse 1."],[35.6,"Numbers 1, 1."],[37.2,"Boring numbers. Before we do that, I'm not gonna do a ton of uh I'm not gonna review all of Leviticus, for example."],[46.16,"But by way of introduction to Numbers, this is also gonna be a lot shorter than a lot of our introductions to books."],[51.92,"Just because bluntly there's less to say about the book of numbers by way of introduction than some of the other books we've been covering."],[60.36,"Partly because a lot of the things that we said all the way back in the introduction to the Torah and the introduction to Exodus and stuff still apply to numbers."],[69.4,"So we've already talked about a bunch of that stuff."],[72.52,"When we get to Deuteronomy, there is a bunch of stuff unique about Deuteronomy."],[79.56,"So that'll have a little beefier introduction again."],[82.52,"But Numbers is sort of this transitional book in a lot of ways."],[87.96,"Part of the way you see that is in the boring name, which the English names are based on the Greek names."],[95.32,"So the name of this book of Greek is Arithme."],[97.8,"Like think arithmetic."],[99.32,"It's literally numbers."],[101.08,"And it's called numbers, presumably because the book ends or begins here at the very first chapter with the first census of the people of Israel, and near the end, not at the end, about six chapters from the end."],[114.4,"But much later in the book, there is a second census."],[117.28,"So presumably because of those two sensi."],[122.32,"They chose the Greek title of numbers for it based on the contents."],[125.84,"The Hebrew name is even more boring, because as we've discussed before, the Hebrew names for books is just the first word."],[134.96,"So the name of this book in Hebrew is Bamidbar, which means in the desert."],[140.32,"So this book is just called in the desert in Hebrew."],[143.56,"Uh, which is is accurate."],[145.2,"It does indeed take place in the desert, but doesn't tell you a lot about what's in it."],[149.96,"So when we talked about Leviticus, we talked about how sort of the core contents of the reason it's called that got that Greek and then Latin title is because it was a book primarily aimed at the Levites, a lot of it, because it talked about sacrificial rituals and their performance and the priests and their ordination, things that the Levites and the priests would need to know to carry out their duties."],[176.6,"Numbers is really picking two chapters."],[179.48,"There's there's two chapters out of 32 here that have to do with censuses, censuses."],[186.52,"Um, and the rest of the book is other stuff."],[190.84,"And part of the problem with categorizing it. it is that's a lot of different stuff"],[198.88,"So there are these two censuses."],[202.8,"There are a big description of the layout of the camp, the Israelite camp in the desert, and where the different tribes camped, and how they processed into and out of camp."],[213.86,"Which is interesting information, but there's that."],[217.14,"Then there's commandments, there's material like that, like we saw in the second half of Exodus and some in Leviticus, but talking about things like inheritance rights and a bunch of other things."],[228.98,"There's just narrative parts, there's just stories of things that happened to the Israelites and to the camp."],[236.02,"And that's just all sort of mixed together."],[238.34,"And there''s nots not. it even grouped together"],[241.18,"It's sort of you have one chapter that's this, and then another chapter that's something completely different, and then another chapter that's something different again."],[249.9,"And so it seems like this this book is sort of put together of a whole bunch of traditions and stories and history that just sort of wasn't in Exodus and Leviticus."],[266.78,"Sort of, it's sort of what's here's what's left."],[269.82,"Okay, and we'll talk more about Deuteronomy when we get to Deuteronomy."],[273.18,"But it doesn't even really function like we'll talk about when we do the intro to Deuteronomy, how Deuteronomy, in some ways, kind of stands by itself."],[283.26,"But if you literally took to itok it out, like if you out of the Torah or the Pentateuch, and so you just had from Genesis to Numbers, Numbers would have a really unsatisfying ending."],[294.42,"It doesn't end in a place that makes any sense."],[297.7,"So it needs something else to be there."],[300.02,"As much as it is kind of this catch-all, kind of an epilogue in some ways, it doesn't really function as an ending."],[308.26,"This is one of the reasons why I made the joke that like nobody when you ask them what their favorite book of the Bible is says numbers."],[314.74,"Because it's just odds and ends."],[316.42,"Now that said, there are a bunch of stories that people know from the Old Testament that are in numbers and that are significant."],[325.7,"So, for example, Moses raising the bronze serpent in the wilderness, that's here in the book of Numbers."],[329.46,"John's Gospel That's important."],[332.56,"Christ refers back to that in St."],[336.24,"So that's a major important story in the book of Numbers."],[339.68,"So it's not that there's nothing here of interest."],[342.88,"It's just that it kind of defies categorization, and there's a lot of different things."],[348.32,"Which makes it a hard book to come up with."],[350.64,"Well, here's a nice introduction to it, because now we're gonna have a bunch of things."],[355.76,"In general, the narrative portions are going to sort of finish the journey of Israel to Canaan."],[366.88,"Meaning by the time we get to the, we're picking up where we ended in Leviticus in terms of the narrative, the history, the story."],[373.28,"And when it ends. they're gonna be camped at the Jordan River getting ready to go into Canaan"],[380.68,"That of course is not gonna actually happen until the book of Joshua, but again, we'll talk more about Deuteronomy when we get to Deuteronomy and why that's there."],[388.6,"So in general, we could say history-wise, it's sort of that leg of the story."],[395.48,"And so most of this, but but this is gonna cover about 40 a little more than 40 years in the desert, which is why I say that Hebrew title kind of works because it at least gives you the setting."],[407.48,"And the things that happened and a bunch of other things."],[411.2,"And how people lived."],[412.84,"So it's it's transitional in a lot of ways."],[415.96,"It's kind of a hodgepodge of different things, but there's a lot of interesting bits."],[418.84,"That's basically my TLDR, my intro to Numbers"],[425.6,"We'll go ahead and get started in Numbers 1, verse 1."],[429.04,"Now the Lord spoke to Moses in the wilderness of Sinai in the tabernacle of Testimony on the first day of the second month in the second year after they came out of the land of Egypt, saying, Take a census of all the congregation of the children of Israel, and according to their families, by their fathers' houses, and according to the number of names, by their head count, every male from 20 years old and above, all who go out with the army of Israel, you and Aaron shall number them by their armies."],[458.12,"And so this is all the time from this is giving us a time reference."],[463.45,"So all the the time from the Exodus and the crossing of Red Sea, everything we've read about between then and here has been two years and two months."],[473.41,"That's how long has passed."],[474.77,"Now, like I said, it's gonna be more than 40 years past during numbers."],[478.53,"So there's this a lopsided time scale."],[480.77,"So everything we've read so far has been at this relatively small amount of time, and now numbers is gonna cross a lot."],[487.49,"And so at this point, God tells Moses remember Moses goes to the tent of meeting to meet with God, speak to him directly, and God says, Go out and basically do a headcount."],[500.77,"The stuff about the fathers's houses, remember how Israel's organized at this point."],[504.53,"You've got the twelve tribes."],[506.45,"Within each of those tribes, you've got clans. which are sort of huge extended families"],[512.25,"And then within each clan, you have extended families, meaning you've got a grandfather or great-grandfather, their sons, their sons, and potentially their sons, depending on how long everybody's been alive."],[527.93,"And those make up the clans, which make up the tribes."],[530.81,"And so that's why I'm sorry, but you work your way down."],[534.01,"You start with the heads of the tribes, you go to the heads of the clans, you go to the heads of the families, and then down to men over the age of 20."],[544.35,"Because as it says, those are the people who, if you're gonna go into battle, those men are the people who are good who you're gonna call up to be in your army."],[553.83,"Because at this point in history, there's not such a thing as standing armies."],[558.55,"Even in major nations like Egypt and stuff, they don't have standing armies."],[563.59,"Just logistically, it was almost impossible to feed a standing army, and you couldn't go to war logistically most of the year, because there weren't ways of dealing with weather and different things."],[575.75,"So there were periods of time when you could go to war and periods when you couldn't."],[578.87,"And so when the time came when you could go to war, if the king was going to go to war, he would call up all of the men over the age of 20, and they would go to war."],[588.23,"So God is saying, get a number, take stock of all those people."],[592.15,"In the process of taking a census, you not only just make tally marks and add up a raw number, but you get to understand who these people are, what tribes they're in, so that if you do need to call them up, you know who you need to contact and where you need to go, and all of these structures, which, if you're talking about thousands of people, Moses wouldn't have known off the top of his head."],[612.83,"Verse 4 with you there shall be each man according to the tribe of each ruler and their father's houses."],[618.91,"These are the names of the men who shall stand with you, from Reuben, Elizer the son of Shedur, from Simeon, Shaluniel the son of Zerushidai, from Judah, Nashon the son of Aminadab, from Issachar, Nethanel, the son of Zuar, from Zebulun, Eliab the son of Helon, from the sons of Joseph, from Ephraim, Elishamah the son of Amahud, from Manasseh, Gamaliel the son of Pedazor, from Benjamin, Abadan the son of Gideon, from Dan Ahiezer, the son of Am Shaddai, from Asher, Pagiel the son of Akran, from Gad, Eliasaph, the son of Duel, from Naphtali, Ahira, the son of Anon."],[662.47,"These were chosen from the congregation, rulers of their father's tribes, and killiarchs in Israel."],[668.15,"Kiliarchs. There we go."],[670.23,"Which means a leader of a thousand."],[672.63,"And remember, thousand just means big number."],[676.07,"When it appears here in ancient text, it's sort of like us saying zillions."],[680.87,"It doesn't mean literally, you know, 999 plus one."],[684.79,"It just means a big group."],[686.79,"The idea here is that. these are the leading men of each of the tribes"],[692.27,"And Moses, when he goes to do this numbering, he's supposed to when he goes to a given tribe, he goes with this person who's the leader, so that they that person knows everybody and is in a position of authority, and so he'll make sure nobody's doing anything funny, trying not to show up, or whatever."],[714.59,"And we've seen Moses is not always the most popular guy in the Israelite camp, so this is also to kind of help with that."],[722.27,"So you have a respected person from each tribe."],[724.75,"So even if the people in a tribe aren't real happy with Moses right now, as they have a habit of getting unhappy with Moses."],[730.95,"They have someone who they respect who will make sure that this. all takes place"],[735.67,"Then Moses and Aaron took these men who had been mentioned by name, and they assembled all the congregation together on the first day of the second month in the second year, and they register registered their ancestry by families, by their father's houses according to the number of names, from twenty years old and above, every male according to their headcount, in the manner the Lord commanded Moses."],[757.51,"So he numbered them in the wilderness of Sinai."],[760.63,"Here we go."],[762.31,"Numbers. You wanted numbers, it does what it says on the tin."],[765.83,"Now there were the children of Reuben, Israel's firstborn son, and their families according to their tribes by their father's houses, and according to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army."],[778.39,"Their census from the tribe of Reuben was forty six thousand five hundred"],[783.39,"There were the children of Simeon and their families according to their tribes by their father's houses, and according to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army."],[794.67,"Their census from the tribe of Simeon was fifty nine thousand three hundred."],[798.91,"There were the children of Judah and their families by their father's houses, and according to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army."],[808.75,"Their census from the tribe of Judah was seventy-four thousand six hundred."],[812.75,"There were the children of Issachar and their families by their fathers's houses."],[816.35,"According to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army, their cens fromus the tribe of Is.sachar was fifty-four thousand four hundred"],[826.71,"There were the children of Zebulun and their families by their father's houses, and according to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army, their census for the tribe of Zebulun was fifty-seven thousand four hundred."],[839.83,"There were the children of Joseph, the children of Ephraim, and their families by their father's houses, and according to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army, their census from the tribe of Ephraim was forty thousand five hundred, and the children of Manasseh and their families by their fathers's houses, and according to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army, their census from the tribe of Manasseh was thirty-two thousand two hundred."],[869.23,"There were the children of Benjamin and their families by their father's houses, and according to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army."],[878.19,"Their census from the tribe of Benjamin was thirty-five thousand four hundred."],[881.95,"There were the children of Gad and their families by their father's houses, and according to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army."],[891.79,"Their census from the tribe of Gad was forty-five thousand six hundred."],[895.55,"There were the children of Dan and their families by their father's houses, and according to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army."],[904.11,"Their census from the tribe of Dan was sixty-two thousand seven hundred."],[907.87,"There were the children of Asher and their families by their fathers' houses, and according to the number of names by their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army, their census from the tribe of Asher was forty-one thousand five hundred."],[920.79,"There were the children of Naphtali and their families by their father's houses, and according to the number of names of their headcount, every male from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go out with the army."],[930.47,"Their census from the tribe of Naphtali was fifty-three thousand four hundred."],[934.71,"This is the census taken by Moses and Aaron, along with the twelve rulers of Israel and their paternal households, each man according to his tribe."],[941.99,"So the entire census of the children of Israel with their armies and from twenty years old and above, all who were able to go to war in Israel was six hundred three thousand five hundred and fifty."],[955.07,"Okay, so we'll pause there after reading all that."],[959.39,"Now we've had this conversation before, but we may be about to have it again, because someone may be wondering how is it possible that they had that many people in the desert?"],[968.51,"Thousand is just a big number."],[970.75,"If these were giving us precise numbers, it would be like 63,512."],[976.11,"Like, so we already know these are rounded, since everything's rounded off to the hundreds, minimally, that this is not trying to give us absolutely precise numbers of people."],[985.63,"I don't know how many different versions of the Bible we have, but certain different versions of the Bible have different numbers."],[991.15,"Because the Greek numbers and the Hebrew numbers aren't the same."],[994.43,"And the reason for that is neither Greek nor Hebrew has numerals."],[999.23,"The reason we call the numerals we use Arabic numerals is because they come from Arabic."],[1003.39,"So when you're writing a number, you use letters"],[1003.99,"So they don't have numerals."],[1007.91,"And based on which letter of the alphabet, like that's what number it is in these ancient texts."],[1013.83,"That causes some weird things, though."],[1016.47,"Because what happens when they use a letter from the second half of the alphabet?"],[1021.35,"Like if it's like one through nine, okay."],[1026.55,"Or in this case, a three digit number of hundreds."],[1029.59,"Is that supposed to be thirteen?"],[1031.59,"Is that supposed to be or is that supposed to be three tens?"],[1035.35,"It's not clear."],[1036.39,"And there are even times in the Old Testament, especially in the Hebrew, where like the letters that they use that we think are trying to give a number, also form a word."],[1046.95,"And so you like you look at you're like, okay, wait, so is this supposed to be a number, or is this supposed to be the word that it spells?"],[1054.91,"And it's not totally clear sometimes."],[1058.43,"So all this is to say, do not take these numbers like super literally."],[1064.27,"But what this does tell us first of all, this gives us an idea of the relative size of the different tribes."],[1070.91,"That gives us that gives us an idea."],[1073.07,"Because even if the numbers aren't precise, we can compare them to each other."],[1077.63,"This tribe is smaller, this tribe is bigger."],[1080.03,"And so one thing you may notice is that the people descended from Joseph, which actually are listed as two tribes here, Manasseh and Ephraim."],[1088.75,"If you put them together, they're bigger than everybody else."],[1092.75,"That's one of the reasons why later in the Old Testament, there are going to be times where they refer to all of Israel or to just the northern kingdom of Israel."],[1102.87,"They just call it Ephraim because that's sort of the biggest group."],[1106.63,"Yeah, there are the other tribes too, but sort of the biggest one is uh descended from Joseph."],[1113.43,"So we get that."],[1114.79,"The other thing is, and we'll have to come back to this in like 25 chapters, but remember there's gonna be that second census that is 40 years later."],[1125.75,"And when we compare those two, we're gonna see that in the 40 years in between, certain tribes will have prospered and certain tribes will have done poorly."],[1135.35,"Again, by comparing the numbers, which doesn't require them to be precise."],[1138.99,"Some are gonna have shrunk, some of them are gonna have gotten bigger, and that's gonna tell us certain things in terms of what's happened in that 40 years, and then what's going to happen following on from that when they go into the land."],[1152.27,"So this is right here at the beginning."],[1155.07,"It's laying groundwork for some things."],[1157.71,"But yeah, don't don't assume that these numbers are like super precise."],[1163.63,"And it just so happened that every tribe had a round number of hundreds of people."],[1169.31,"Of men. Remember, this is men, this is just men over 20."],[1173.55,"So if you throw in women and children, this would be like a million more than a million people."],[1177.49,"So again, this is we're not this is not precise."],[1182.05,"Verse 47. But the Levites from their father's tribe were not numbered with the children of Israel"],[1188.17,"For the Lord spoke to Moses saying, See to it that the tribe of Levi is not numbered, nor take a census of them among the children of Israel."],[1196.33,"But you shall appoint the Levites over the tabernacle of testimony over all its furnishings and over all things that belong to it."],[1202.89,"They shall carry the tabernacle and all its furnishings, they shall minister liturgically in the tabernacle and camp around it."],[1209.69,"This is important because this sets out right off the bat the Levites, which includes the priests, families of priests and the high priest family, do not do military service."],[1222.41,"They are not called up to fight."],[1224.41,"So these two things are being established as incompatible."],[1227.85,"Going out making war, even when God is commanding that war"],[1232.37,"Even if it's a defensive war that God allows for in his commandments, that kind of bloodshed and priest the priesthood dealing with the holy things is incompatible."],[1243.49,"This is why eventually David's gonna get told he can't build the temple."],[1247.97,"Because he spent his life at war."],[1251.49,"Not just as king commanding people to go to war, but I mean he spent a chunk of his life as a mercenary, like the head of a mercenary unit, going and fighting for the Philistines."],[1259.99,"He's literally a warrior."],[1261.51,"And so, no, warrior is a thing, and even though David's a man after God's own heart, he's a warrior."],[1268.31,"Warrior doesn't do temple things."],[1270.95,"These are kept separate."],[1272.15,"Now, this, of course. is true, like, throughout the history of the Orthodox Church"],[1276.99,"Not always true in the West, but in the history of the Orthodox Church, Orthodox priests are forbidden to bear arms."],[1285.23,"And if a priest is responsible for somebody's death, you can no longer serve as a priest."],[1290.11,"And that's not just like killing someone in a war or even killing somebody in self defense."],[1296.43,"That's if I were to kill somebody in a car accident, I could no longer serve as a priest."],[1301.55,"If you're responsible in any way for shedding human blood, you can no longer serve as a priest because these two things are fundamentally incompatible."],[1311.39,"And that's to provide I mean we tend to think about this individualistic kind of level of well, that's not fair to some priesthood."],[1317.95,"That's not the level we'll get right"],[1320.95,"God's trying to communicate something to them about priesthood and about holiness and about the importance of peace."],[1328.63,"Like pay attention during the Great Litany sometime about how many of the petitions do we ask for peace?"],[1334.55,"It's like five or six out of like twelve were asking for peace."],[1339.43,"And we we come from a culture that is not that dissimilar from like Roman, pagan Roman culture in that we tend to value victory more than peace."],[1353.11,"We say, Well, there will be peace once we've won."],[1355.67,"We'll win, and then we will declare peace, which is like the Pax Romana."],[1359.59,"Livey said the Romans created a wasteland and called it peace."],[1362.23,"it peace Isn'tful now that we've killed everything?"],[1362.79,"But that's kind of how we view it"],[1367.95,"As soon as we win, as soon as I win, there will be peace."],[1371.39,"There will be no need to fight anymore, because I will have won."],[1375.79,"But that's that's counter to what God is communicating already here in the Torah."],[1381.55,"Then that gets made very explicit in the course of the New Testament, where Christ says, Blessed are the peacemakers, blessed are the people who come to two fighting parties and not figure out who's right and help them win, but who come and make peace between the two parties."],[1396.19,"So, and not only, so they don't, they're exempt from the military service because they're serving the tabernacle."],[1402.81,"We read all about it being built and dedicated and everything a couple of times in the second half of Exodusus and in Levitic."],[1407.45,"And they also camp around it"],[1409.25,"So that's their job."],[1413.33,"Now, we've talked about this before, but we're gonna start getting into now the way the camp was laid out."],[1420.37,"So, part of the importance of this, we talked about how within the camp, and then the camp itself where the Israelites are, there's sort of these this series of concentric circles of holiness that have to be maintained."],[1433.25,"Because God is present in the tabernacle in the middle."],[1437.09,"So the closer you are to where God is, the higher a level of holiness you have to maintain."],[1444.05,"So the high priest, on that one day when he's gonna go into the Holy of Holies where God is, the most holy place in the town. he's got to maintain like the highest level of holiness on that day"],[1456.33,"And then the priests, when it's their time to go into the holy place to minister, they have the next highest level of holiness they have to maintain."],[1464.73,"When they're not doing that, when it's not their duty, then they could be with their wives and have kids and do all the other things."],[1471.21,"But when the time comes for their they have to be totally dedicated to that and maintain this higher level of holiness, and then the Levites and priests in general, and then compared to the rest of the world, the Israelites themselves are all supposed to maintain a higher level of holiness than the other nations."],[1488.41,"Notice I said supposed to."],[1490.17,"And then you go out to outside the camp, the world, which is not all that holy at all."],[1495.53,"And so part of the reason why they're exempt from military service here, these ideas are connected, is that they are camping, they are living right around the tabernacle."],[1507.33,"It's not just that they go to work in the tabernacle, but they are living closer to God."],[1514.61,"And so they have to have this higher level of holiness to do that."],[1520.13,"And so things like the layout of the camp and where they're physically camped, this is trying to communicate this larger idea."],[1529.25,"That the closer we want to draw to God, the higher a level of holiness that requires of us."],[1536.93,"Meaning what? Meaning, if we want to draw closer to God, the way to do that is repentance."],[1543.45,"I can't stay the way I am and living the way I am and just expect to become closer to God without me changing it all."],[1551.77,"Because what is that doing?"],[1553.77,"That's me remaining in my sinfulness and my uncleanness and demanding that God come to me."],[1558.41,"Well, that's not gonna happen."],[1560.41,"And if it did happen, it would be bad news for me."],[1563.61,"If God showed up and I'm sinful and unclean."],[1566.65,"That's not gonna go so well for me."],[1568.49,"I don't actually want that."],[1570.41,"And so if we want to draw closer to God, we want to come to know more God more fully."],[1575.37,"The way to do that is we have to repent."],[1577.85,"And repentance doesn't just mean saying I'm sorry, but we have to actually change how we're living and live in a holier way, and then we will draw closer to God, where God is."],[1587.37,"We could come closer to him"],[1587.89,"He doesn't need to move."],[1591.01,"But so this is that God is teaching this to them in this sort of this very literal way of physical space."],[1597.97,"They're physical. Those people, they live closer to the tent where God lives in their tents, so they have to be holy."],[1605.66,"That's a way of communicating to late Bronze Age people."],[1608.88,"This more spiritual idea through a very literal sort of sign."],[1615.52,"Verse 51 When the tabernacle is to move, the Levites shall dismantle it."],[1620.88,"And when the tabernacle is to make camp, the Levites shall set it up."],[1625.28,"The foreigner who comes near, let him be put to death."],[1629.2,"So again, this is the idea, this is a sacred responsibility to take down and put up the tabernacle, the holy things of the tabernacle."],[1639.4,"It's this responsibility of them to handle these things."],[1642.44,"This is not something that just anybody can come do."],[1645.74,"We're not taking volunteers, and especially not somebody who isn't living their life to the standard that these people are required to live their life to that, to that standard."],[1658.86,"Because when that happens, you're taking the things of God, and you're making them common."],[1664.78,"You're treating them just like common things."],[1667.34,"Like we we don't treat the chalice that we receive the Eucharist from the same way I treat this muck."],[1673.18,"And if I took it and I used it the way Ied use this mug, you would all be shocked and appall."],[1676.06,"the If I walked in here one Bible study with coffee in community."],[1683.27,"And I would not be a priest for very long because someone would tell the bishop."],[1687.03,"I'm not accusing you of being stoolies."],[1689.03,"I just that would be the right thing to happen in that case, would be for you to tell the bishop I was doing that."],[1693.91,"But so you can't take the things of God and treat them like they're just common things."],[1701.19,"Things will not go well with you."],[1703.19,"And so just having anybody, and so foreigner here, the idea is this is the most extreme example."],[1711.11,"It's not just like, oh, an Israelite who's not a Levite."],[1714.87,"Or my cousin who's half Levite."],[1717.27,"It's the most extreme example."],[1718.67,"Someone who's not even in the camp."],[1720.31,"Just some random guy, some Kennesite who we found wandering around in the desert."],[1725.07,"We invited him to come, you know, help put up the tabernacle."],[1729.47,"Because that would be sort of the pinnacle of just making it common."],[1732.35,"Like, oh no, just anybody can do this."],[1734.11,"It's not a big deal."],[1737.23,"Okay, verse 52."],[1738.67,"But the children of Israel shall make camp with their armies, each man according to his own rank, and each man according to his own regiment."],[1745.55,"However, the Levites shall camp around the tabernacle of testimony in the Lord's presence, and there may be no sin among the children of Israel."],[1753.63,"And the Levites themselves shall guard the tabernacle of testimony."],[1757.47,"Thus the children of Israel did according to all the Lord commanded Moses and Aaron, so they did."],[1763.07,"So what's being re-emphasized there. there when it says shall be no sin among the children of Israel is again they're called to this higher level of holiness"],[1772.23,"By extension, the Levites were even higher."],[1775.59,"But there shall be no sin means multiple things."],[1781.11,"So at one level, there shall be no sin means, yeah, don't commit sins."],[1787.03,"Don't violate any of the commandments you've been given."],[1789.27,"And they've already been given a lot."],[1790.71,"There's gonna be a few more in numbers that they're going to be given."],[1794.15,"But obviously, that's going to happen."],[1796.07,"We know that's going to happen because we just read Leviticus."],[1799.03,"So we just read about all the sin offerings and guilt offerings and trespassings."],[1804.91,"right Right? All of all of the things that God has instituted to deal with when those things happen."],[1810.31,"So it is not going to be a surprise to God when people sin in Israel"],[1817.47,"So part of there shall be no sin in Israel, sin here is being used as a noun."],[1825.87,"It's not a verb, it's not a participle."],[1828.43,"So it's not there shall be no sinning in Israel."],[1830.75,"It's there shall be no sin in Israel."],[1833.39,"So if someone violates a commandment and they repent, meaning usually they make restitution first."],[1843.63,"So I steal my neighbor's scythe."],[1846.51,"I ask to borrow it, I don't give it back."],[1848.83,"I have to go and pay him back for it plus extra because I stole."],[1854.91,"So if I violated the commandment against stealing, I stole something, I make the restitution I'm required to make, and then I go and I offer a sacrifice, then that sin doesn't remain in Israel."],[1871.51,"It's gone. It's taken care of."],[1874.55,"It's dealt with."],[1876.87,"So part of what this is saying, there shall be no sin in Israel, is not just you better not break any of these rules."],[1885.33,"That's not that's not even the main thing."],[1888.13,"The main thing is you can't allow sin to remain."],[1891.33,"It needs to be dealt with when it happens."],[1894.29,"And if someone refuses to repent, as we've seen in Exodus of Leviticus, and these commandments. someone refused to repent, they need to be removed from the community"],[1904.49,"Physically exiled, or in extreme cases, the death penalty."],[1908.57,"And those were seen as similar things."],[1912.65,"In the extreme case where someone just will not repent of what they and they are going to keep doing what they're doing."],[1920.09,"And so this principle is all through, if you remember when you're going through them, all through St."],[1926.57,"Paul's letters in the New Testament in terms of church communities."],[1930.33,"So, like, remember in 1 Corinthians, he writes to the church in Corinth, there's a man there who has been sleeping with either his mother-in-law or his stepmother."],[1941.45,"It's not totally clear."],[1943.93,"And this this is ob isv obiouslyvious sexual immorality to pretty much even to pagan Greeks, pretty not right."],[1945.21,"But from what St"],[1952.77,"Paul says in 1 Corinthians, not only were the other people in the Christian community in Corinth not doing anything or saying anything about it, they were kind of patting themselves on the back about how sort of tolerant and open-minded they were."],[1966.45,"And St. Paul is like, no, that's not how this works."],[1971.41,"Uh, you can't let this sit and fester."],[1974.77,"And one of the things, points St."],[1976.53,"Paul makes is that that's not loving to that man."],[1980.93,"You guys think you're being so kind and loving and forgiving and open-minded because you just let him do this."],[1988.53,"But that's not."],[1989.57,"Paul basically says"],[1989.93,"If you love him, you'll throw him out."],[1992.57,"That's what St."],[1994.73,"If you love him, you'll do whatever it takes to get him to repent."],[1998.65,"Before it's too late for him to repent."],[2001.53,"Because this is destroying him."],[2003.21,"But if you allow it to happen in your community without saying anything, it's gonna not only destroy him, it's gonna destroy your whole community."],[2010.81,"And by you not saying anything, you're kind of aiding and abetting."],[2014.25,"You're kind of an accessory in our modern legal terms to what he's doing."],[2018.73,"Because you know about it and you're saying nothing."],[2020.89,"And so he's gonna present excommunication, sort of exile."],[2026.02,"The same kind of thing, as for the person who wrote repent, it's the ultimate call for them to repent."],[2032.02,"Like, look, maybe this, and he'll even refer to it as handing them over. to Satan"],[2035.66,"Why? Well, who's who who controls that area outside the camp?"],[2040.62,"We talked about the the Day of Atonement, Azazel, the you know, the devil right out there in the world."],[2048.38,"And he'll sit refer to that as turning them over to Satan, meaning put them out there in the world, let them see what that's like."],[2055.1,"And hopefully that will shock them, get them to come to their senses and come back and repent."],[2062.06,"And when they do that, you need to be ready to forgive and restore them."],[2065.58,"Once they're really repenting, then they're leaving that sin behind."],[2069.18,"So that's what that's getting at here."],[2071.1,"There can't be sin."],[2072.06,"You can't just, but God has given means for dealing with it."],[2077.58,"So it's not just, oh, you broke a commandment, you're out."],[2079.82,"That's not how it works"],[2080.82,"Oh, you broke a commandment, we're stoning you to death."],[2084.82,"That that kind of thing is only for the person who is completely unrepentant and just refuses, refuses to repent, refuses to make restitution, refuses to do what they need to do after multiple attempts."],[2096.58,"That's the principle that's getting laid down here."],[2099.22,"What that also means, the other place this principle is important, is we're gonna find places as we go forward in the old testament, and even in the new testament, where it's gonna talk about people who kept the Torah, kept the commandments perfectly."],[2118.42,"And in some cases, you're just gonna go, wow, like, is that possible?"],[2123.14,"But there are some places where you're gonna get really confused."],[2124.82,"Because one of those people is David"],[2130.14,"After King David has died, when God sends a prophet to Jeroboam, the son of Nebat, who's the first king of the northern kingdom of Israel, he says, I will do for you what I did for David and give you a dynasty without end, if you, like David, keep all of my commandments and walk in all of my ways."],[2149.58,"And you're flipping back a few pages, going, Okay, wait a minute, like he killed a guy to cover up committing adultery with his wife and getting her pregnant?"],[2159.5,"Like, what? But if you understand this principle, you know David repented."],[2165.52,"He repented, he did what was required of him to repent."],[2168.4,"He accepted responsibility."],[2169.36,"We'll get there to the Bible study eventually"],[2170.04,"We get this picture of the story of David."],[2173.88,"This beautiful picture of what real repentance from serious sin looks like."],[2178.36,"But he did that."],[2179.72,"And so in the eyes of God, once he had repented like that, he had kept all of his commandments."],[2189.56,"Because some of those commandments in Leviticus are about what to do when you sin."],[2195.4,"David kept those too."],[2197.88,"He kept the commandments about what to do when he sinned."],[2201.4,"But that's important."],[2203.0,"This is a very important principle."],[2204.6,"This runs against the way a lot of us were taught the first five books of the Bible."],[2210.28,"A lot of people were taught this as if it's like this with these really, you know, this angry old Testament. God makes all these rules, and if you break any of them, you get killed"],[2219.42,"Either he smites you or you know, the community kills you, stones you to death."],[2225.58,"And that's not not only is that not how it worked in reality, that's not what's here on the page either, if you read it closely."],[2236.62,"Next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2243.42,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancient faith.com."],[2248.78,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.8,"lv":2,"h":"Introduction to Numbers: a short one","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting at the beginning of <b>Numbers 1:1</b> - \"boring numbers\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Intro <b>shorter</b> than most books</li><li class=\"d1\">little to add: Torah and Exodus intros <b>still apply</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Deuteronomy</b> has unique stuff → \"beefier\" intro later</li><li class=\"d0\">Numbers = a <b>transitional book</b> in many ways</li><li class=\"d0\">English name from the Greek: <i>Arithmoi</i> = \"think arithmetic\" - literally <b>numbers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">named for the <b>first census</b> (ch. 1) and the <b>second census</b> about six chapters from the end</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek title chosen from the <b>contents</b></li></ul>"},{"t":125.84,"lv":3,"h":"Hebrew name: Bamidbar","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew book names = just the <b>first word</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Bamidbar</i> = <b>\"in the desert\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">accurate - it does take place in the desert</li><li class=\"d1\">but tells you little about what is in it</li></ul>"},{"t":149.96,"lv":2,"h":"A catch-all: what is in Numbers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Leviticus: Greek/Latin title because it was aimed at the <b>Levites</b> - sacrifices, priests, ordination</li><li class=\"d0\">Numbers: only <b>2 chapters of 32</b> are censuses - the rest is \"other stuff\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hard to categorize - a mix of:</li><li class=\"d1\">two <b>censuses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">big description of the <b>camp layout</b> - where tribes camped, how they processed in and out</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>commandments</b> like second half of Exodus and Leviticus - e.g. <b>inheritance rights</b></li><li class=\"d1\">plain <b>narrative</b> - stories of what happened to Israel and the camp</li><li class=\"d0\">Not grouped: one chapter this, next chapter completely different</li><li class=\"d0\">Looks like <b>traditions, stories, history</b> that weren't in Exodus and Leviticus</li><li class=\"d1\">\"here's what's left\"</li></ul>"},{"t":273.18,"lv":3,"h":"No real ending; but not empty","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Deuteronomy \"stands by itself\" in some ways - more at its intro</li><li class=\"d0\">Genesis to Numbers alone → Numbers has an <b>unsatisfying ending</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ends where nothing makes sense → needs something more</li><li class=\"d1\">a catch-all, kind of an <b>epilogue</b>, but doesn't function as an ending</li><li class=\"d0\">Nobody names Numbers as their <b>favorite book</b> - \"odds and ends\"</li><li class=\"d0\">But well-known stories are here</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Moses raising the bronze serpent</b> in the wilderness</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ refers back to it in St. John's Gospel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not nothing of interest - it just \"defies categorization\"</li></ul>"},{"t":355.76,"lv":3,"h":"Where the narrative goes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Narrative portions <b>finish Israel's journey to Canaan</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Picks up where <b>Leviticus</b> ended</li><li class=\"d0\">Ends with Israel <b>camped at the Jordan</b>, ready to enter Canaan</li><li class=\"d1\">actually entering = <b>Joshua</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Covers a little more than <b>40 years in the desert</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew title works: gives the <b>setting</b></li><li class=\"d1\">and what happened, how people lived</li><li class=\"d0\">\"TLDR\": <b>transitional hodgepodge</b>, with interesting bits</li></ul>"},{"t":425.6,"lv":2,"h":"Numbers 1:1-3 - Take a census","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Lord speaks to Moses in <b>wilderness of Sinai</b>, tabernacle of testimony</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>first day of second month, second year</b> after Egypt</li><li class=\"d0\">Command: census of all the congregation</li><li class=\"d1\">by <b>families, fathers' houses, number of names</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>every male 20 and up</b> - all who go out with the army</li><li class=\"d1\">Moses and Aaron to number them <b>by their armies</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Time reference: <b>2 years 2 months</b> since Exodus and Red Sea</li><li class=\"d1\">Numbers will span <b>40+ years</b> → \"lopsided time scale\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses goes to the <b>tent of meeting</b>, speaks to God directly</li></ul>"},{"t":500.77,"lv":3,"h":"Why men over 20: no standing armies","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Israel's structure:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>12 tribes</b> → <b>clans</b> (huge extended families) → <b>extended families</b> (grandfather, sons, their sons)</li><li class=\"d1\">census works down: heads of tribes → clans → families → men over 20</li><li class=\"d0\">Men over 20 = those you'd <b>call up for battle</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No <b>standing armies</b> then, even in Egypt</li><li class=\"d1\">logistically impossible to <b>feed</b> one</li><li class=\"d1\">couldn't fight most of the year - <b>weather</b></li><li class=\"d1\">king calls up all men over 20 when the season comes</li><li class=\"d0\">A census is more than tally marks</li><li class=\"d1\">learn <b>who they are, which tribe</b> → know who to contact and where</li><li class=\"d1\">Moses \"wouldn't have known off the top of his head\"</li></ul>"},{"t":612.83,"lv":2,"h":"1:4-16 - The tribal leaders","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">One man from each tribe stands with Moses</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Reuben</b> - Elizur; <b>Simeon</b> - Shelumiel; <b>Judah</b> - Nahshon son of Amminadab</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Issachar</b> - Nethanel; <b>Zebulun</b> - Eliab; <b>Ephraim</b> - Elishama; <b>Manasseh</b> - Gamaliel</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Benjamin</b> - Abidan; <b>Dan</b> - Ahiezer; <b>Asher</b> - Pagiel; <b>Gad</b> - Eliasaph; <b>Naphtali</b> - Ahira</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Chiliarchs</i> = leader of a <b>thousand</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"thousand\" = just a <b>big number</b> - like us saying \"zillions\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not literally 999 + 1; just a big group</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses goes to each tribe <b>with its leader</b></li><li class=\"d1\">leader knows everyone, has authority</li><li class=\"d1\">makes sure nobody <b>\"does anything funny\"</b>, skips out</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses \"not always the most popular guy\" in camp</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>respected person</b> from each tribe → census happens even if they're unhappy with Moses</li></ul>"},{"t":735.67,"lv":2,"h":"1:17-46 - The census figures","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moses and Aaron assemble the congregation, register ancestry <b>by families</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Does what it says on the tin\" - the numbers:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Reuben</b> 46,500; <b>Simeon</b> 59,300; <b>Judah</b> 74,600</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Issachar</b> 54,400; <b>Zebulun</b> 57,400</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ephraim</b> 40,500; <b>Manasseh</b> 32,200</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Benjamin</b> 35,400; <b>Gad</b> 45,600; <b>Dan</b> 62,700; <b>Asher</b> 41,500; <b>Naphtali</b> 53,400</li><li class=\"d0\">Total: <b>603,550</b> men able to go to war</li></ul>"},{"t":955.07,"lv":3,"h":"Numbers are rounded, not literal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q anyone will ask: how that many people in the desert?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Thousand\" = big number</b>; precise would be like 63,512</li><li class=\"d1\">already <b>rounded to hundreds</b> minimum - not absolutely precise</li><li class=\"d0\">Bibles differ: <b>Greek and Hebrew numbers aren't the same</b></li><li class=\"d1\">neither language has <b>numerals</b> - \"Arabic numerals\" come from Arabic</li><li class=\"d1\">numbers written with <b>letters</b></li><li class=\"d1\">later-alphabet letter: 13? or 3 tens? - \"not clear\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes the letters also <b>spell a word</b> → number or word?</li><li class=\"d0\">→ \"do not take these numbers <b>super literally</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Men only, over 20 → with women and children, <b>more than a million</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1064.27,"lv":3,"h":"What the numbers do tell us","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Relative size</b> of tribes - can compare even if imprecise</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Joseph's descendants</b> = two tribes, <b>Ephraim + Manasseh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">together bigger than all the rest</li><li class=\"d1\">why later OT calls all Israel / the northern kingdom <b>\"Ephraim\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Second census</b> 40 years later (~25 chapters on)</li><li class=\"d1\">compare: some tribes <b>prospered</b>, some <b>shrank</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tells what happened in 40 years and what follows entering the land</li><li class=\"d0\">Laying groundwork here at the start</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Every tribe had a round number of hundreds\" - rounded</li></ul>"},{"t":1182.05,"lv":2,"h":"1:47-50 - Levites are not numbered","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Tribe of Levi <b>not numbered</b> with the children of Israel</li><li class=\"d0\">Appointed over the <b>tabernacle of testimony</b>, its furnishings</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>carry</b> the tabernacle and furnishings</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>minister liturgically</b>, camp around it</li><li class=\"d0\">Sets out: <b>Levites, priests, high priest's family do no military service</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>War and priesthood incompatible</b> - even war God commands, even a defensive war God allows</li><li class=\"d1\">bloodshed vs. dealing with the holy things</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>David</b> can't build the temple</li><li class=\"d1\">spent his life at war - king commanding armies, and <b>head of a mercenary unit</b> fighting for the Philistines</li><li class=\"d1\">\"man after God's own heart\" yet a <b>warrior</b> → \"warrior doesn't do temple things\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1272.15,"lv":3,"h":"Orthodox priests and bloodshed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">True through Orthodox history - \"not always true in the West\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox priests <b>forbidden to bear arms</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Responsible for a death → <b>can no longer serve</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not only war or self-defense</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>car accident</b> counts</li><li class=\"d1\">any responsibility for <b>shedding human blood</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We think individually - \"not fair\" - wrong level</li><li class=\"d0\">God communicating about <b>priesthood, holiness, peace</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1328.63,"lv":3,"h":"Peace versus victory","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Great Litany</b>: about 5-6 of 12 petitions ask for <b>peace</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Our culture like <b>pagan Roman</b>: values <b>victory</b> over peace</li><li class=\"d1\">\"peace once we've won\" = <b>Pax Romana</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Livy: Romans \"created a wasteland and called it peace\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"as soon as I win, there will be peace\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Counter to what God says already in the Torah</li><li class=\"d0\">Made explicit in NT: <b>\"Blessed are the peacemakers\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not figuring out who's right and helping them win → <b>making peace</b> between the parties</li><li class=\"d0\">Levites exempt because they serve the tabernacle - built and dedicated in Exodus and Leviticus - and <b>camp around it</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1413.33,"lv":2,"h":"Camp layout: circles of holiness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Camp layout = <b>concentric circles of holiness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God present in the <b>tabernacle</b> at the center</li><li class=\"d1\">closer to God → <b>higher holiness</b> required</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>High priest</b>: highest level, the one day in the <b>Holy of Holies</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Priests</b>: next level when ministering in the holy place</li><li class=\"d1\">off duty: with wives, kids, \"all the other things\"</li><li class=\"d1\">on duty: totally dedicated</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Levites and priests</b> → then <b>Israelites</b>, higher than other nations - \"notice I said <i>supposed to</i>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Outside the camp = the world, \"not all that holy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Levites exempt because they <b>live right around the tabernacle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just work there - live closer to God</li><li class=\"d0\">Camp layout communicates: <b>closer to God = higher holiness</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1529.25,"lv":3,"h":"Drawing closer means repentance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Draw closer to God → <b>repentance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can't stay as I am and \"expect to become closer\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that = remaining in <b>sinfulness and uncleanness</b>, demanding God come to me</li><li class=\"d1\">if it did happen → \"bad news for me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Repentance isn't just <b>saying sorry</b> - <b>change how we live</b>, holier</li><li class=\"d0\">We come closer; <b>God doesn't need to move</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God teaching \"late Bronze Age people\" a spiritual idea through <b>literal physical space</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1615.52,"lv":2,"h":"1:51 - Levites set up and take down the tabernacle","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Tabernacle moves → <b>Levites dismantle</b>; camps → Levites set up</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"The foreigner who comes near shall be put to death\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sacred responsibility - \"not taking volunteers\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not someone not living to that standard</li><li class=\"d0\">Otherwise <b>making the things of God common</b></li><li class=\"d1\">chalice vs. coffee <b>mug</b>: bring coffee to Bible study in the chalice → \"would not be a priest for very long\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"someone would tell the bishop\" - and that would be right</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Foreigner</b> = most extreme example</li><li class=\"d1\">not just a non-Levite Israelite or \"my cousin who's half Levite\"</li><li class=\"d1\">some random <b>Kenizzite</b> wandering in the desert invited to help</li><li class=\"d1\">pinnacle of \"anybody can do this, no big deal\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1737.23,"lv":2,"h":"1:52-54 - \"There shall be no sin\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Israel camps <b>each by rank, regiment</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Levites camp around the tabernacle, in the Lord's presence, <b>\"that there may be no sin among the children of Israel\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Levites <b>guard</b> the tabernacle</li><li class=\"d1\">Israel did all as commanded</li><li class=\"d0\">Higher holiness for Israel, <b>higher still</b> for Levites</li><li class=\"d0\">\"No sin\" means multiple things</li><li class=\"d1\">first: don't violate the commandments - already many, a few more coming in Numbers</li><li class=\"d1\">but Leviticus: <b>sin offerings, guilt offerings</b> - God instituted ways to deal with it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not a surprise to God when people sin\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1817.47,"lv":3,"h":"Sin as a noun: don't let it remain","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Sin\" here a <b>noun</b>, not a verb - not \"no sinning\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Violate a commandment → <b>repent</b>, usually <b>restitution first</b></li><li class=\"d1\">steal neighbor's <b>scythe</b> → pay back plus extra</li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>offer a sacrifice</b> → sin \"doesn't remain in Israel\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"It's gone. Taken care of.\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Main point: <b>don't let sin remain</b> - deal with it when it happens</li><li class=\"d0\">Refuse to repent → <b>removed from the community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">physical <b>exile</b>, extreme cases <b>death penalty</b> - seen as similar</li></ul>"},{"t":1920.09,"lv":3,"h":"St. Paul and 1 Corinthians 5","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Principle runs through <b>Paul's letters</b> for church communities</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1 Corinthians</b>: man sleeping with mother-in-law or <b>stepmother</b> - \"not totally clear\"</li><li class=\"d1\">obvious immorality even to pagan Greeks</li><li class=\"d1\">Corinthians \"patting themselves on the back\" for being <b>tolerant, open-minded</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"that's not how this works\" - can't let it <b>fester</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>loving</b> to him: \"if you love him, you'll throw him out\"</li><li class=\"d1\">do whatever it takes to get him to repent <b>before it's too late</b></li><li class=\"d1\">silence destroys him and the community - you're an <b>accessory</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Excommunication</b> = sort of exile = the ultimate call to repent</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>handing over to Satan</b>\" - outside the camp is his domain</li><li class=\"d1\">cf. <b>Day of Atonement, Azazel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hope: shock them to come to their senses</li><li class=\"d0\">When they repent → <b>forgive and restore</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"broke a commandment, you're out\" or stoned</li><li class=\"d1\">only the <b>completely unrepentant</b>, after multiple attempts</li></ul>"},{"t":2099.22,"lv":3,"h":"David kept all the commandments","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Later OT / NT: people who <b>kept the commandments perfectly</b> - sometimes \"is that possible?\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>David</b>: God to <b>Jeroboam son of Nebat</b> - dynasty \"if you, like David, keep all my commandments\"</li><li class=\"d1\">flip back: killed a man to cover adultery with his wife?</li><li class=\"d0\">Principle explains it: <b>David repented</b>, accepted responsibility</li><li class=\"d1\">beautiful picture of <b>real repentance from serious sin</b> - \"we'll get there\"</li><li class=\"d1\">some Leviticus commandments are about <b>what to do when you sin</b> - David kept those too</li><li class=\"d0\">Runs against how many were taught the Pentateuch</li><li class=\"d1\">\"angry Old Testament God\" - break any rule, get <b>smitten or stoned</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not how it worked, and \"not what's here on the page\" if read closely</li></ul>"}]});
